# A committed TEMPLATE of .github/c2-serving-job.env for the NP9 long-answer window (tp4/np9-answer32k; scripts/ci/c2_serving_job.py parses it): copy it over
# .github/c2-serving-job.env on a throwaway commit of tp4/np9-answer32k and push a tag experiment/c2-serving-vN, ONE TAG AT A TIME (wait for the run to finish; never cancel a running card job).
# Nothing here names a rig, a card, a host or a registry: the cards are resolved at run time. The jobs are templates only: no tag is pushed from the branch by its author. ORDER.txt has the
# order, the stop rules, the minutes and the read rules.
# ONE IMAGE FOR THE WHOLE WINDOW: C2_IMAGE_TAG=tp4-np9-1 is a PLACEHOLDER for the tag B0 builds from the pushed branch head WITH the K64j-OQ graft (sed -i 's/tp4-np9-1/<tag>/' *.env). A commit of a file
# the image carries needs a new B0 build and a new tag in every template. This window never stops, starts or hands back the node agent.
# C2_MODEL_REPO / C2_MODEL_REVISION (qwen_c2_checkpoint.py): a job that carries them serves the Swift checkpoint with its own converted-weight directory (never the base's) and, since NP9, the output ceiling of its
# catalog entry (qwen_c2_checkpoints.json "request": output_budget 32768, default_max_tokens 16384). A job without them is the base: its ceiling is the profile's 16,384, so the long-answer tests would read as clamped.
# N3: EIGHT requests at once, prompts of about 96,000 tokens (as the server counts them), 30,000 tokens each (ignore_eos), on Swift. The longest prompt at which eight
# 30,000-token requests are all resident: 8 x 1,971 = 15,768 of the 16,499 blocks (eight 30,000-token requests fit up to 101,872-token prompts, eight at the full 32,768
# ceiling up to 99,104; docs/tp4-np9-answer32k.md), so no seat waits: the container log holds NO "kv reservation hold" line (a hold here means the pool arithmetic is
# wrong). The eight prefills queue (about 26 s each), then eight seats decode at depth 96,000 to 126,000 together for 30,000 tokens. CEILING READ: the container log holds
# "[QWEN-C2] request output budget 32768 for this checkpoint (its catalog entry; the profile says 16384)" and "[QWEN-C2] request contract installed: greedy, max_tokens <=
# 32768" (c2_smoke_check requires the second whenever a long-answer test ran), the checkpoint lines (c2_smoke_check --expect-checkpoint) and no "kv reservation too large"
# line. ANSWER READ (c2_smoke_check, long_answer_problems): every user of a long_answer test ends with finish "length" and exactly 30,000 tokens (ignore_eos, so the stop is
# the ceiling, not the model); fewer tokens at "length" is a server whose ceiling clamped the request. MEMORY READ: the output ceiling allocates no verifier capture,
# proposal bucket or page table (docs/tp4-np9-answer32k.md), so the DRAM lines are the ship-4 profile's own: no "dram backstop refused", no "dram admission hold" that the
# same prompts would not have drawn at 16,384, no engine-build failure, no "audit mismatch", no stall. READ also: the per-seat rate at depth is recorded against
# concurrent8_code_128k_long of the Swift window, never gated. RUN WITH THE CI PAUSED (a model load under CI load has hit the platform's load timeout; the decodes here run
# 7 to 30 minutes). TELEMETRY (C2_TELEMETRY=1, scripts/ci/card_telemetry.py): the read-only ARC sidecar samples every chip once a second through the smoke step; the first
# [TELEMETRY] line must say decode check ok. A 30,000-token decode is minutes at the board power limit: a rate that falls while AICLK falls is the clock, not the ceiling.
# Duration (estimate): 80 minutes.
C2_CARDS=quad
C2_ACTIONS=reset smoke
C2_IMAGE_TAG=tp4-np9-1
C2_PROFILE=c2-packed-tp4-8x262k-ship-prefix-levern-w2-er-fx-traffic-s4
C2_MODEL_REPO=ukisai/Swift-1.5-Qwen3.8-27b
C2_MODEL_REVISION=b4c84d42903a8646b25857eb2827288d92ed85a4
C2_SMOKE_TESTS=warmup,concurrent8_steady,long_answer8_96k
C2_TELEMETRY=1
C2_BOX_MINUTES=150
